The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah. Their mother was Tamar. Perez was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salem was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. David was the father of Solomon. Solomon's mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon's father was Rehoboam. Rehoboam's father was Abijah. Abijah's father was Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Jehoram. Jehoram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jeconiah and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After this, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Salathiel. Salathiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zorubbabel was the father of my father Oeel. Oeel was the father of my father Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of my father Azor.
Azor was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Aaim. Aaim was the father of Eliud.
Elimelech was the father of Elnathan. Elnathan was the father of Ara. Ara was the father of Rakeen. Rakeen was the father of Shallum. Shallum was the father of Jediael. Jediael was the father of Josabad. Josabad was the father of Zeror. Zeror was the father of Abigail. Abigail was the mother of Amasa.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 from David until the Jews were forced to walk to Babylon. There were 14 from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary and Joseph had promised to get married. But before they started to live together, it became clear that she was going to have a baby. She became pregnant by the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Joseph was a righteous man. He didn't want to expose her publicly. That's why he decided to divorce her slowly.
And when Joseph considered doing this, an angel from the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said to him, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. The child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
To have a son, you must give him the name Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this happened so that what the Lord had said would come true. He had said it through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and she will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God is with us."
Sufian woke up. He did what the angel of the Lord told him to do. He took Mary home as his wife.
He did not have a loving relationship with her until after she gave birth to a son. And Joseph named him Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place during the time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, the wise men from the east came to the city of Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be the king of the Jews? For we saw his star when we were in the east, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and all in Jerusalem were equally troubled.
Herod called together all the chief priests of the people. He also sent for the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered. "For this is what the prophet has written. 'He said,
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Subsequently, Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them exactly when the star appeared.
He sent them to Bethlehem. He said to them, "Go! Search diligently for the child. And when you find him, bring me word, so that I too may go and worship him."
After the Magi had heard the king, they went on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They knelt down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense and myrrh.
But their Lord warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. The family of Jesus flees to Egypt
When the wise men had gone, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Get up!" the angel said. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to search for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. At night, he walked to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. In this way, the word spoken by the Lord through the prophet was fulfilled. He had said, "I have surely called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod understood that the wise men had deceived him. Because of this, he became very angry. He gave orders regarding Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the young boys who were two years old or less were to be killed. This was in accordance with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. He said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of lamentation and deep mourning. Rachel weeps for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod died, Joseph had a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Joseph stood up. He took the child and his mother Mary and they returned to the land of Israel.
Therefore, when he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea, having ruled in place of his father Herod, this made Joseph afraid to go there. In a dream, he was warned, so he returned to the region of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. This is what the prophets said about Jesus that came true. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the desert, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothing was made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to Him from Jerusalem, and all Judea, and from the region beyond the Jordan.
In order to confess their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan.
John saw a large number of Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Show forth fruits, showing that you have repented of your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is lying at the roots of the trees. All fruitless trees that do not bear good fruit shall be cut down. They will throw them into the fire.
I baptize you with water, and I baptize you to repent of your sins. As for me, one is coming who is mightier than I am, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
Here is the translation of the provided text: "Shukto is in his hand to clean the straw from his threshing floor. To gather his wheat into the granary. But to burn the straw with unquenchable fire." Jesus was baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
John tried to stop him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus replied, "Leave it as it is for now. It is right to do so. This fulfills God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
When Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove upon him.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, and I love him. I am very pleased with him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, Satan tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of not eating, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the Temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you kneel down and worship me," he said, "I will give you all this."
Jesus said to him, "Away from me, Satan! For it is written, 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Afterwards, the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John the Baptist was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus walked from Nazareth. He walked to live in the city of Capernaum. It was on the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, what the prophet Isaiah said would come true. He said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jewish people live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territories east of the Jordan River!
The people who walk in darkness will see a great light. They who dwell in the land of deep darkness will see a great light. But for others, the light will only shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time, Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. He saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing fishing nets into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow Me, said Jesus, so that I can make you fishers of men.
At that very moment, they left their nets and followed him.
Going along, he saw two other brothers. They were James, son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. As they were preparing their nets, Jesus called to them.
As they got out of the boat, and their father, they followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus walked in all Galilee. There he was teaching in the synagogues. He was preaching the good news of the kingdom of God. He was healing all sickness and every disease among the people.
The news about him spread throughout all of Syria. People brought him everyone who was sick with all different kinds of diseases. Some of them were suffering from intense pain. Others were possessed by demons. Some of them were shaking uncontrollably. And others were completely unable to move at all. And Jesus healed everyone.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region across the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on a mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
After that, he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are those who spiritually hunger. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Congratulations to those who are sad. They will find comfort.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, that they might be merciful.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Congratulations to those who suffer because they do what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Congratulations to you, when people make fun of you and hurt you because of me. Congratulations to you also, when they tell all kinds of evil lies about you because of me.
Be happy and pleased. Your reward in heaven is great. In the same way, people hurt the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but will be thrown out and trampled underfoot by people.
You are the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden.
Furthermore, people don't light a lamp and put it under a glass. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then it shines on everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
You may not think that I have come to abolish the scriptures or the prophets. I have not come to do that. On the contrary, I have come to complete the true meaning of what is written.
What I am going to tell you is true. Heaven and earth will pass away before the smallest mark of the law is made invalid. Not the smallest stroke of a pen will disappear from the law until everything is completed.
Do not break even one of the least important commandments. And do not teach people to break them. If you do that, you will be called the least person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here's what I can tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven.
Hear what they said to the people who lived long ago. They said to them, "You shall not kill. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever kills will be subject to judgment."
