He told the people around him, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod imprisoned John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John the Baptist. But he was afraid of the people, because they thought that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. Herod was very pleased with her.
He promised her, by oath, to give her anything she asks for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What should I ask for?" This is what the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large platter."
The king was very angry, but he thought of his promise and of his guests at the supper, so he said to one of his officers to give her what she asked for.
Herod cut off John's head in prison.
His head was pulled off a large plate and given to the girl. Then she took it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took away his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. He went in a boat to a quiet place. The people heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came ashore, he saw a large crowd and was deeply moved by their need. He healed their sick.
As the evening was almost come, the scholars came to him. "There is nothing here," he said to them. "It is already late. Disperse the people. They can go and buy some food in the villages."
Jesus replied, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They said: "We had five loaves of bread and two fish."
"Get them for me here," he said.
Then Jesus called the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and thanked God. He broke the bread into pieces. Then he gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and rested. The students collected 12 baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was about 5000. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately, Jesus had the disciples get into the boat. He let them go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then, he sent the crowd away.
After dismissing them, he went up alone to the side of a mountain to pray. As the evening came, he was alone walking.
The chart was already too far from the land. The waves were beating against it, because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were very afraid. "It's a ghost!" they cried. And they screamed in terror.
At that moment, Jesus called out to them, "Be strong! I am. Don't be afraid."
"Oh Lord, is it You?" Peter asked. "If it is You, tell me to come to You on the water."
"Come," said Jesus. Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
When Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Meanwhile, Jesus reached out his hand and grasped him. He said to him, "Your faith is exceedingly small! Why have you doubted Me?"
As they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then the people in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Gennesaret.
The men who had lived there, you will get to know Jesus. So they sent a message throughout the nearby countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They asked him to let the sick touch even the fringe of his cloak. And all who touched it were made clean.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They asked,
Why don't your students obey what the elders teach? Your students don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus replied, "Why do you not obey the command of God? You wish to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will surely be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
As for you, you allow people to tell their parents, "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Thus, they should not honor their parents with their gift. You make the Word of God ineffective to follow your own teachings.
O protesters! Isaiah was right to prophesy about you. He said,
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human commandments." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to him. He said, "Listen and understand.
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." But what proceeds out of your mouth, that is what makes you "unclean."
Afterward, the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?"
Jesus replied, "The plants my Father in heaven did not plant will be pulled up by the roots.
Let them be. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man guides a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Haven't you understood yet? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that goes into the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
The things that come out of the mouth, they come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. Also theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
Here are the things that make you "unclean." However, eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus walked from Galilee and walked to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan who was living near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is demon-possessed and she suffers greatly."
Jesus didn't say a single word. His disciples came to him. They sought his help, "Send her away to leave. She's always screaming behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel. They are like a sheep that has wandered off."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
He replied, "It is not right to take the bread of the children and throw it to the dogs."
"Oh, my goodness," she said. "Even the dogs get to eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that very moment. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus walked away from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought with him blind people and people who could not walk. They also brought paralytics, people who could not speak, and many other people. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. The disabled became healthy. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind could see. Thus the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for they might faint on the way."
His disciples answered him. "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we find enough bread to feed all these people?"
Jesus asked, "How much bread do you have?" They replied, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Take the seven loaves of bread and the fish, and give thanks. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples gathered seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 40,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the people away, he got into the boat. He went to the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
Answer, "In the evening you will see in the sky. You say, 'The weather will be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and cloudy." She knows the meaning of what she sees in the sky. But she cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; but no sign shall be given to it but the sign of the prophet Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to bring the bread.
"Be careful," Jesus said to them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked about this among themselves. They said, "He must be saying this because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said to them, "Your faith is very small! Why are you discussing with each other that there is no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you think that five loaves are for five thousand? Don't you think how many baskets you have gathered from the pieces?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the 4000? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How can you not understand? I wasn't talking to you about bread. But be careful of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Afterwards, the disciples understood that Jesus did not mean to warn them about the yeast used in bread. He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus walked into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "The one who is spoken of by John the Baptist. And others say Elijah. And others say Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"And you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus replied, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
Here's what I can tell you. You are Peter. To this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to tear it down.
To give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Afterwards, Jesus warned his disciples again not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time, Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them, "It is necessary for Me to go to Jerusalem. There, I must suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. I must be killed, and on the third day, I must rise from the dead."
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Lord, my God!" he said. "This shall never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You do not have the concerns of God in mind. On the contrary, you are concerned with the affairs of people."
After this, Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone would come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.
If he loves to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What's the point of someone gaining the whole world and losing themselves? Or what could possibly change their soul?
The Son of Man is coming in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will reward each person according to what he has done.
What I am about to tell you is true. Some of the people standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was transformed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He brought them up onto a high mountain. They were completely alone.
Before them, his form changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothing became white like light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
As Peter was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the students heard this, they were terribly frightened. They fell face forward to the ground.
Jesus came and touched them. He said to them, "Arise, do not be afraid."
When they went up, they saw no one; only Jesus was there.
They came down from the mountain. On the way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "That's right. Elijah is supposed to come and restore everything again."
As for me, I tell you, Elijah has come, and people do not recognize him. They have done to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will also do to the Son of Man."
Afterwards, the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus. He knelt down in front of him.
"Oh Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He shakes violently and suffers greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought it for your students. But they can't cure it.
People, you are wicked and godless! Jesus replied. How long must I be with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the child here to me.
Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to come out of the boy, and it came out of him. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and asked, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He replied, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
