They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is to be delivered over to the hands of men."
"To fulfill what the prophets said: 'He will take bread, bless it, break it, and give it to them.' Then he said, 'This is my body, which is for you. Do this to remember me.' Then he took a cup, gave thanks to God, and gave it to them. He said, 'This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is shed for you.' But behold, the hand of the one betraying me is with the hand of mine on the table. And they said to him, 'Is it you, then, my lord?' And he said, 'It is I. But do not do the thing that you are contemplating to do to me, in that hour, in that hour of darkness and blindness which is coming upon the sons of men, in order that they may grope for the wall, and as they pass along they will stretch out their hands, and will grope as if they were the blind. And many of the people, hearing this, said, 'Truly, this is the Prophet. Truly, this is the Prophet, whom God is about to send into the world.' But others said, 'No, for a prophet does not come from Galilee, does not a prophet come from Galilee?' And there arose a division in the crowd because of him. And some of them wanted to seize him, but no one laid a hand on him. And the officers stood there, having made a strict arrest. And one of the bystanders said to them, 'Why do you stand here? If you had seized him, you would have had to bring him before the governor. And you would not have to do that.' And they let him go, and returned to the palace. And as they passed along, they saw a man blind from birth. And they asked him, 'Rabbi, who sinned, this man or his parents, that he should be born blind?' Jesus answered, 'Neither this man nor his parents sinned; but this happened so that the works of God might be displayed in him. As long as it is day, we must do the works of him who sent me. And night is coming, when no one can work. While I am in the world, I am the light of the world.' When he had said this, Jesus spat on the ground and made mud with his spit. Then he anointed the man's eyes with the mud. And he said to him, 'Go, wash in the pool of Siloam.' And so he went and washed, and came back seeing. The neighbors and the people who had seen him before as a beggar, said, 'Is not this the man who used to sit and ask for alms?' Some said, 'This is he,' but others said, 'No, but he is like him.' But Jesus said to them, 'You are wrong at the beginning of the matter. For you have the testimony of John, but you are unwilling to receive his testimony. You yourselves send messengers to Jerusalem, saying, "We do not see any sign of John." And you yourselves are the evidence, because you have not believed his testimony. I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.'" When John, who was in prison, heard about the deeds of the Messiah, he sent a message to him through his disciples and said, "Are you the one who is to come, or should we look for someone else?" And Jesus said to them in reply, "Go and tell John what you have seen and heard: the blind see, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised, and the poor have the good news preached to them. Blessed is anyone who does not stumble because of me." And as they went away, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind? What then did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft clothing? Look, those who wear soft clothing are in kings' houses. What then did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet. This is the one about whom it is written, 'Behold,
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Canaan. Then the tax collector came to him and asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, that's how he does it," he replied. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth collect customs and taxes? Do they collect it from their children or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children ought not to perish," Jesus told him.
We don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find exactly the coin you need. Take it and give it to them for my taxes and yours.
